In the weak magnetic field,

$$
\begin{aligned}
H & = \frac{p^{2}}{2m} + V(r) + \frac{eH}{2mc}\left(L_{z} + 2S_{z}\right) + \xi(r) \boldsymbol{S} \cdot \boldsymbol{L} \\
& = H_{0} + \frac{eH}{2mc} S_{z}
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose $\left(J^{2}, L^{2}, j_{z}\right)$ as the common eigenstates

$$
H_{0} \psi_{nljm_{j}} = \left(E_{nlj} + m_{j} \frac{eH\hbar}{2mc}\right) \psi_{nljm_{j}}
$$

Since the magnetic field $H$ is very weak, the term $\frac{eH}{2mc} S_{z}$ can be treated as a perturbation. Let $\psi_{nlm_{j}} = \psi_{nlm_{j}}(r, \theta, \varphi)$. Its angular part of the wave function is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_{ljm_{j}} &= \sqrt{\frac{j+m_{j}}{2j}} \alpha Y_{j-\frac{1}{2}, m_{j}-\frac{1}{2}} + \sqrt{\frac{j-m_{j}}{2j}} \beta Y_{j-\frac{1}{2}, m_{j}+\frac{1}{2}} & j=l+\frac{1}{2} \\
\varphi_{ljm_{j}} &= -\sqrt{\frac{j-m_{j}+1}{2j+2}} \alpha Y_{j+\frac{1}{2}, m_{j}-\frac{1}{2}} + \sqrt{\frac{j+m_{j}+1}{2j+2}} \beta Y_{j+\frac{1}{2}, m_{j}+\frac{1}{2}} & j=l-\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

where $\alpha, \beta$ are the eigenstates of $S_{z}$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\frac{\hbar}{2}$ and $-\frac{\hbar}{2}$. Thus,

$$
\left\langle jm_{j}\right| \sigma_{z}\left| jm_{j}\right\rangle = 
\begin{cases}
\frac{m_{j}}{j}, & j=l+\frac{1}{2} \\
-\frac{m_{j}}{j+1}, & j=l-\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{eH}{2mc} \overline{S_{z}} = \frac{eH\hbar}{4mc} 
\begin{cases}
\frac{m_{j}}{j}, & j=l+\frac{1}{2} \\
-\frac{m_{j}}{j+1}, & j=l-\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases} \\
E_{nlm_{j}} = E_{nlj} + \frac{eH\hbar}{2mc} 
\begin{cases}
\left(1+\frac{1}{2j}\right) m_{j}, & j=l+\frac{1}{2} \\
\left(1-\frac{1}{2j+2}\right) m_{j}, & j=l-\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases} \\
\Delta E = 
\begin{cases}
\mu_{B}H\left(1+\frac{1}{2j}\right), & j=l+\frac{1}{2} \\
\mu_{B}H\left(1-\frac{1}{2j+2}\right), & j=l-\frac{1}{2}
\end{cases}
\end{gathered}
$$

Reference answer:
\boxed{\mu_{B}H\left(1+\frac{1}{2j}\right), \mu_{B}H\left(1-\frac{1}{2j+2}\right)}

